---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy Policy - Plannotator"
  description="How Plannotator stores local review data and when its optional features contact hosted services or third parties."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground">
    <Nav />
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 pt-20 pb-16">
      <article class="prose">
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Last updated: October 24, 2026</p>

        <p><strong>Plannotator sends no usage data except optional daily counts for experimental features you turn on</strong>, described in <a href="#usage-counts">section 5</a>. Plans, documents, diffs, annotations, drafts, history, and configuration stay on your machine by default. Some features contact GitHub, your Git remote, a URL you ask Plannotator to fetch, an AI provider, or a hosted sharing service. Those boundaries are described below.</p>

        <h2>1. What this policy covers</h2>
        <p>This policy covers the open source Plannotator application, <a href="https://plannotator.ai">plannotator.ai</a>, <a href="https://share.plannotator.ai">share.plannotator.ai</a>, the hosted short-link service, the usage-counts service at <code>counts.plannotator.ai</code>, and the Workspaces waitlist in this repository.</p>
        <p>It does not control third-party AI providers, Git hosts, websites you fetch, services through which you send a share link, or self-hosted deployments. Those services apply their own terms and privacy practices.</p>

        <h2>2. Data that stays local by default</h2>
        <p>The open source application runs a local server and opens the review UI in your browser. It stores Plannotator-managed files under <code>~/.plannotator</code> by default, or under <code>PLANNOTATOR_DATA_DIR</code> when configured. These files can include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>plan and annotate version history</li>
          <li>annotation drafts and saved review decisions</li>
          <li>a durable archive of the feedback you submit, under <code>~/.plannotator/feedback/</code></li>
          <li>configuration, session records, guide history, and diagnostic logs</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The feedback archive is on by default. Every plan decision, code review submission, and annotate submission is recorded locally so a review is not lost when an agent times out or a terminal is closed. Each record holds your own review text, the document or code excerpts it quotes, and annotation metadata; it does not hold the diff itself. Nothing prunes the directory, so records are kept until you delete them. Set <code>PLANNOTATOR_FEEDBACK_HISTORY=0</code> to never write it, and delete <code>~/.plannotator/feedback/</code> (or one project subdirectory inside it) to forget what is there. Like the rest of this data, it stays on your machine and is never sent to the Plannotator project owner.</p>
        <p>Some UI preferences are stored as functional browser cookies. Plannotator never sends this local review data to the Plannotator project owner. The usage counts in <a href="#usage-counts">section 5</a> are numbers only and hold no review content.</p>

        <h2>3. Automatic network requests</h2>

        <h3>GitHub release check</h3>
        <p>Plannotator checks whether a newer version exists by sending one HEAD request to GitHub's public "latest release" page (<code>github.com/backnotprop/plannotator/releases/latest</code>), reading the version from where it redirects, at most about once every ten minutes per computer, and when the Inbox starts or its page is reloaded (at most once a minute). Only when that page cannot be reached does it ask GitHub's public latest-release API instead, at most once an hour (when GitHub answers that its rate limit is reached, Plannotator waits until the time GitHub names, up to six hours, or six hours when it names none). When it finds a newer version, the Inbox also asks the API once for that release's notes. The request comes from the Plannotator program on your computer, not from the review page in your browser. It carries no plan, document, diff, annotation, version number, or usage data, and the Plannotator project owner receives no analytics from it. GitHub receives an ordinary web request and may process information such as your IP address and user agent under GitHub's privacy practices. To turn the check off, set <code>PLANNOTATOR_UPDATE_CHECK=0</code>, add <code>&#123; "updateCheck": false &#125;</code> to <code>~/.plannotator/config.json</code>, or switch off "Tell me when a new version is out" in Settings. Running <code>plannotator update</code> always checks, because you asked it to.</p>

        <h3>Installer downloads</h3>
        <p>The install scripts download the selected Plannotator binary and checksum from GitHub Releases. When installing the latest version, they first query GitHub's API to resolve the release tag. If available, a token from <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, <code>GH_TOKEN</code>, or <code>gh auth token</code> is sent to that API. Pinning <code>--version</code> skips only the release-resolution API call; the binary and checksum still come from GitHub Releases.</p>
        <p>A full install can also clone Plannotator from GitHub to install agent integrations, download the optional <code>sem</code> sidecar from the Ataraxy-Labs/sem GitHub repository, ask Pi to install <code>@plannotator/pi-extension</code> from npm, run <code>npx skills add</code> when you select extra skills, and use npm to install the managed agent-terminal runtime. Provenance verification, when enabled, also contacts GitHub through <code>gh</code>. Minimal mode and the applicable skip flags omit their corresponding optional components.</p>

        <h3>Git remote checks during local code review</h3>
        <p>For a local Git review, Plannotator may run <code>git ls-remote origin</code> in the background to identify the remote default branch and tell you when the local baseline is behind. This contacts the Git host configured as <code>origin</code>, but does not send the local diff. Choosing the in-app Fetch action runs <code>git fetch</code>.</p>

        <h2>4. Features that send data when used</h2>

        <h3>URL annotation and Jina Reader</h3>
        <p>When you ask Plannotator to annotate a URL, Plannotator requests that URL. Public URLs may first be requested directly for content negotiation and, when Jina is enabled, the URL is also sent to Jina Reader so Jina can fetch and convert it. If Jina fails or is disabled, Plannotator fetches the URL directly. Local and private-network URLs skip Jina. The requested site or Jina receives normal request metadata, and a configured <code>JINA_API_KEY</code> is sent to Jina.</p>

        <h3>GitHub and GitLab reviews</h3>
        <p>Reviewing a hosted pull request or merge request uses your authenticated <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code>, and Git configuration to fetch the diff, metadata, comments, checks, repository content, and related remote refs. Plannotator can also query author avatars and refresh review discussions. Posting review comments or changing provider-side viewed state happens only through the corresponding review actions. GitHub, GitLab, or your self-hosted forge processes these requests.</p>
        <p>In local Git commit review, Plannotator may also look up commit-author avatars. For GitHub remotes it fetches recent commit metadata and matches returned email addresses locally; it does not send the local commit-author email to GitHub for that match. For GitLab remotes it sends commit-author emails to the configured GitLab instance's avatar endpoint: one email per request, up to ten in parallel per lookup round; overflow is retried in later rounds. If an avatar is found, your browser requests the returned image URL from GitHub, GitLab, Gravatar, or another image host.</p>

        <h3>Ask AI and review agents</h3>
        <p>Ask AI, Guided Review, Code Tour, and review agents use an AI provider or agent CLI that you select and have already configured. A request can include your question, selected text, and relevant plan, document, repository, or diff context. Plannotator does not route this AI content through a Plannotator-operated model proxy, but the selected provider may process or retain it under your account and that provider's policy. Claude Ask AI allows WebSearch by default, so a question can also send a search query to Anthropic's search service and return third-party web results.</p>
        <p>Review-agent permissions depend on the selected engine. Claude allows file-reading tools and Git or forge command patterns intended for inspection, while denying direct file-writing tools, WebFetch, WebSearch, and general-purpose shells; some allowed Git and forge patterns are broader than a strict read-only subcommand list. Copilot denies its direct write tool and specific high-risk version-control and forge operations, allows Git and forge command families, and auto-denies other shell tools. Codex runs with <code>--approve-for-me</code>, which uses its workspace-write sandbox and automatic approval review. Cursor runs in ask mode with its sandbox enabled by default unless you opt out. OpenCode's plan agent and Pi retain shell access under their own CLI and runtime controls; Pi removes its direct edit and write tools but does not remove Bash. Plannotator tells every engine not to modify files or post comments, but those prompt instructions are not the same as structural enforcement.</p>

        <h3>Share links</h3>
        <p>Open source asynchronous link sharing remains available for compatibility but is moving to deprecated support. Workspaces is the primary direction for team sharing. No removal date has been announced.</p>
        <p><strong>URL-fragment shares:</strong> For markdown shares that fit in a URL, Plannotator compresses the shared plan and annotations into the fragment after <code>#</code>. This content is compressed, not encrypted. Browsers do not include the fragment in the HTTP request to the share portal, so the portal host does not receive the shared content through that request. The browser still requests the portal page, and its host receives ordinary request metadata. Anyone or any messaging service that receives the full link can read or retain the shared content.</p>
        <p><strong>Encrypted short links and raw HTML:</strong> For markdown, clicking Create short link confirms the upload. Plannotator encrypts the share payload with AES-256-GCM before uploading ciphertext to the paste service. The decryption key is placed in the URL fragment and is not included in the paste or portal HTTP request. Anyone with the complete link can decrypt the content. The hosted paste service expires ciphertext after seven days.</p>
        <p>Raw HTML cannot use a hash-only share. In a local raw-HTML session, invoking the header's Copy Share Link action or a configured Approve or Send Feedback callback action can create and upload the encrypted short link immediately, without the markdown Create short link confirmation. A remote raw-HTML session creates the encrypted short link automatically at session creation so the HTML can be opened from another machine. In remote mode, markdown sessions still print a hash-based link.</p>

        <h3>Callback-enabled links and remote resources</h3>
        <p>A share-link creator can choose any <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code> callback endpoint and include a token. If you use the link's Approve or Send Feedback button, the browser posts the action, token, and annotated share URL to that endpoint. For an encrypted short link, <code>annotated_url</code> can contain the complete URL, including the fragment decryption key. Treat the link creator and callback endpoint as trusted with the shared content and key. Prefer HTTPS because HTTP does not provide transport encryption.</p>
        <p>Plans, documents, raw HTML, and hosted PR descriptions can reference remote images, video, fonts, or other assets. Your browser may request those resources from their hosts when it renders them. The app bundles its default fonts and syntax-highlighting assets, but selecting a hosted code-review font can request Google Fonts or jsDelivr. Opening an in-app help video loads a privacy-enhanced YouTube embed; YouTube receives the request and may store or access data when you interact with the video.</p>

        <h2 id="usage-counts" class="scroll-mt-20">5. Usage counts for experimental features</h2>
        <p>Plannotator has experimental features. Each one is off until you turn it on. When you turn one on in Plannotator's settings, Plannotator shows a notice: it says that Plannotator will count how often you use that feature, lists every count, and says how to turn the counts off. Counts are on once you have seen that notice.</p>
        <p>Plannotator records counts for a feature only while all of these are true:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>you turned the feature on in Plannotator's settings (a feature turned on only with an environment variable is never counted);</li>
          <li>you have seen the notice for that feature;</li>
          <li>you have not turned counts off;</li>
          <li>the feature's counting end date has not passed.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Development and source builds of Plannotator record and send nothing, unless a developer points them at a test server on their own computer.</p>

        <h3>Which features are counted</h3>
        <p>One feature is counted: <strong>Decision mode</strong> in the Plannotator Inbox, until April 30, 2027. For each day, Plannotator counts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>whether the Inbox was open with decision mode on;</li>
          <li>whether the Decide button showed (two or more questions waiting);</li>
          <li>how many times you opened decision mode;</li>
          <li>how many questions you answered in it;</li>
          <li>how many replies you sent from it;</li>
          <li>how many times you left it to open a thread;</li>
          <li>how many times you reached the end with nothing left.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Plannotator also works out two facts on your computer and sends them as plain values: whether that day was the first day this computer used decision mode (sent once), and how many of the last 7 days you used it, as a range (1, 2&ndash;3, 4&ndash;5 or 6&ndash;7).</p>

        <h3>What is sent, and when</h3>
        <p>For each feature and each UTC day on which something was counted, the Plannotator program on your computer (never your browser) sends one request after that day has ended, at a random time up to 4 hours later. The request holds the feature's name, its version number, the date, and the counts above as whole numbers, each capped at 100. Its headers are fixed, so every computer sends the same bytes for the same counts. Run <code>plannotator telemetry --show</code> to print exactly what would be sent.</p>
        <p>If your computer was offline or Plannotator was not running, the days still waiting are sent together later: at most 14 requests at a time, a few seconds apart. A day older than 3 days is deleted without being sent.</p>
        <p>Plannotator never sends your questions, answers, messages, files or code; paths, project, repository or agent names; the Plannotator version; or any identifier for you or your computer.</p>

        <h3>Where it goes, and what is kept</h3>
        <p>The requests go to our server on Cloudflare, <code>counts.plannotator.ai</code>.</p>
        <p><strong>In transit, requests can be linked.</strong> Every request carries your IP address, as any network request does, and Cloudflare sees it while delivering the request. Cloudflare's rate limiter also uses it to allow at most 60 requests a minute from one address. Requests that arrive from the same IP address, for example several days sent together after you were offline, could be tied to each other and to that address while they are in transit. The random delay and the gaps between requests make this harder but do not prevent it.</p>
        <p><strong>At rest, we keep only totals.</strong> Our server adds each request's numbers to daily totals across everyone. It stores no IP address, no time finer than the day, and no record of a single request, person or computer, and it keeps no log of requests.</p>
        <p><strong>Small totals.</strong> When only a few computers report on a day, a stored total can hold the numbers of a single computer. Nothing in the table says which computer that was. The interface we use to read totals shows only days that can no longer change (older than 3 days), only days with at least 5 reporting computers, and only figures that at least 5 computers contributed to. We, the operator, can also open the table directly through our Cloudflare account, where small totals are visible as stored.</p>
        <p><strong>Database history.</strong> Cloudflare keeps a history of the database's writes for 7 days (30 days on a paid plan), so an earlier state of the totals can be restored. Comparing two states a moment apart could, in principle, show the numbers one request added. We use this history only to undo mistakes, never to look at single requests, and it expires on its own.</p>

        <h3>Purpose and legal basis</h3>
        <p>We count use of experimental features to decide whether to keep, change or remove them. Our legal basis is our legitimate interest in improving Plannotator with as little data as possible (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)). We weighed that interest against yours: only whole numbers for one feature and one day are sent, nothing that identifies you is stored, and you can stop at any time.</p>
        <p>You have the right to object. Turning counts off (below) is an objection that takes effect at once on your computer. You can also write to the contact in <a href="#contact">section 10</a>.</p>
        <p>Cloudflare, Inc. runs the server as our processor, under its data processing addendum. Cloudflare may process the requests, including your IP address, outside your country, including in the United States.</p>

        <h3>How long it is kept</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>On your computer, a day's counts that were not sent within 3 days are deleted unsent.</li>
          <li>On our server, a feature's totals are deleted 180 days after its counting end date, and no total is kept longer than 400 days.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Turning counts off</h3>
        <p>Run <code>plannotator telemetry --off</code>, or set <code>PLANNOTATOR_TELEMETRY=0</code> or <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code>. Turning a feature off also stops its counts. No setting or environment variable can force counts on.</p>
        <p>When counts are off, Plannotator deletes the counts not yet sent, the list of days you used the feature, and its local copy of what was sent. Two things stay on your computer: your choice to turn counts off, in Plannotator's settings file; and, for each feature you used, the fact that it was used (with no date), so the "first day" fact is never sent twice.</p>
        <p><code>plannotator uninstall</code> deletes all local count files. It keeps your settings file, including a choice to turn counts off, so that choice still holds if you reinstall. <code>plannotator uninstall --purge</code> deletes the settings file too.</p>

        <h3>Your rights</h3>
        <p>You have the right to access, rectify, erase or restrict data, and to object (GDPR Arts. 15&ndash;18 and 21). Our server holds no identifier, so we cannot find the totals you contributed or tell them apart from anyone else's (GDPR Art. 11). You can lodge a complaint with your data protection supervisory authority.</p>

        <h2>6. Website and Workspaces waitlist</h2>
        <p>Our websites and hosted services use infrastructure such as AWS, Amazon CloudFront, and Cloudflare. These providers may process IP addresses, request headers, timestamps, and similar delivery or security metadata. The marketing site loads fonts from Google Fonts, contributor avatars from GitHub's image hosts, a release badge from Shields.io, repository information through GitHub's API, and privacy-enhanced YouTube embeds on some blog posts. Opening those pages sends ordinary requests to the corresponding third parties. YouTube may also store or access data when you interact with an embedded video.</p>
        <p>We do not run advertising trackers, usage analytics, or analytics cookies on these sites. The usage counts in <a href="#usage-counts">section 5</a> come from the Plannotator program on your computer, never from a website. Functional cookies may remember theme, review settings, and a dismissed update notice.</p>
        <p>Workspaces is a separate hosted product and the primary team-sharing direction. The local-storage statements for the open source application do not describe Workspaces. This public repository contains the current Workspaces waitlist, not the hosted product implementation.</p>
        <p>If you join the waitlist, we store the information you submit: email address, company, team size, optional note, and whether you identify as a contributor. If you leave company blank and use a non-free email domain, the service can infer a company name from the domain and records that the value was inferred. The service also records IP address, country, user agent, and referrer for rate limiting, abuse prevention, and signup context. Cloudflare Turnstile processes a verification token and related request data. We use waitlist data to manage beta access, contact you about Workspaces, and cross-reference contributor status when requested.</p>

        <h2>7. Retention</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Local Plannotator files remain on your machine until you delete them or use the applicable cleanup controls.</li>
          <li>Hosted encrypted short-link ciphertext expires after seven days. A self-hosted operator can configure a different period.</li>
          <li>Usage-count totals are deleted 180 days after a feature's counting end date, and are never kept longer than 400 days (<a href="#usage-counts">section 5</a>).</li>
          <li>Workspaces waitlist entries are retained while needed to run the waitlist, contact applicants, and keep basic signup records. You can request deletion.</li>
          <li>Support emails are retained as needed to respond and keep a record of the conversation.</li>
          <li>Infrastructure and third-party providers may retain request or security data under their own policies.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>8. Your choices</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Set <code>PLANNOTATOR_SHARE=disabled</code> to hide the open source sharing controls.</li>
          <li>Set <code>PLANNOTATOR_JINA=0</code> or use <code>--no-jina</code> to prevent URL annotation from using Jina. The requested URL is still fetched directly.</li>
          <li>Set <code>PLANNOTATOR_AI=disabled</code> to disable the in-app AI and review-agent surfaces.</li>
          <li>Set <code>PLANNOTATOR_FEEDBACK_HISTORY=0</code> to stop archiving submitted feedback locally, or delete <code>~/.plannotator/feedback/</code> to remove what has been archived.</li>
          <li>Set <code>PLANNOTATOR_REVIEW_PROGRESS=0</code> to stop code review saving which files you marked viewed (records include repository file paths), or delete <code>~/.plannotator/review-progress/</code> to remove saved progress.</li>
          <li>Self-host the share portal and paste service, or do not use link sharing.</li>
          <li>Do not send a share link through a service you do not trust with the content or key in that link.</li>
          <li>Run <code>plannotator telemetry --off</code>, or set <code>PLANNOTATOR_TELEMETRY=0</code> or <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code>, to turn off usage counts for experimental features.</li>
          <li>Set <code>PLANNOTATOR_UPDATE_CHECK=0</code>, or switch off "Tell me when a new version is out" in Settings, to stop the automatic GitHub release check.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>9. Security</h2>
        <p>Client-side encryption limits what the paste store receives, but it does not protect a link after you share it. Treat the complete URL as the secret. Compression is not encryption, so hash-only links must also be shared only with trusted recipients and services. A callback recipient can receive a complete annotated short-link URL, including its decryption key, so use only a trusted callback target.</p>
        <p>No internet service or client-side application can guarantee perfect security. For sensitive or regulated work, review the source and your provider policies, disable optional network features, and self-host where appropriate.</p>

        <h2 id="contact" class="scroll-mt-20">10. Contact and privacy requests</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Michael Ramos</strong><br />
          Email: <a href="mailto:backnotprop@gmail.com">backnotprop@gmail.com</a>
        </p>
        <p>You may ask for access to, correction of, or deletion of personal information we hold about you. We generally cannot identify or recover hash-fragment content, local files, or encrypted paste content from the decryption key because we do not receive that key.</p>

        <h2>11. Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>We may update this policy when Plannotator's behavior or hosted services change. The date at the top identifies the current version.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <Footer />
  </div>
</Base>
